          "text": "introns. Alternatively, introns may be nonfunctional sequence remnants left over from the fusion of ancient genes\nthroughout the course of evolution. This is supported by the fact that separate exons often encode separate protein\nsubunits or domains. For the most part, the sequences of introns can be mutated without ultimately affecting the\nprotein product. All of a pre-mRNA\u2019s introns must be completely and precisely removed before protein synthesis. If the process errs\nby even a single nucleotide, the reading frame of the rejoined exons would shift, and the resulting protein would be\ndysfunctional. The process of removing introns and reconnecting exons is called splicing (Figure 15.13).",
